og text contains \"vivid\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Jerusalem","confidence":0.95}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Between about 640 and 1100, Muslims transformed Christian Jerusalem—especially the Haram al-Sharif—physically and ideologically to embody early Islam, shaping architectural achievements like the Dome of the Rock.","confidence":0.75},"academicOrientation":{"score":60,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.7,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"The Dome of the Rock","confidence":0.65},{"name":"Haram al-Sharif","confidence":0.7}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"354d4f0a09d9fa6611a53d600482750ab3c602c6f6144bf3dbf67a309747c74f","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"History","secondarySubjects":["Religion"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores the historical transformation of Jerusalem during the early Islamic period, with a strong focus on the architectural developments and religious significance of the era.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"549f56e03447f13bffdbe8f4858a36ea450424fb1b974390a8d86e1493396741","summary":"From the time of Herod through the Crusades, Jerusalem had officially \"changed its religion\" several times, with Jews, Christians, and Muslims inscribing the story of their faiths on the urban landscape. In this handsomely illustrated book, noted Islamist Oleg Grabar offers a rare account of the great role played by early Islam in defining the \"look\" of Jerusalem that remained largely intact until the twentieth century. From about 640 to 1100, Muslims transformed Christian Jerusalem, mainly the area now known as the Haram al-Sharif, both physically and ideologically to embody their new faith. Grabar examines this process, showing how it led to great architectural achievements, including The Dome of the Rock, still perhaps the most vivid image to impress any visitor to Jerusalem. Offering a major photographic record of The Dome's mosaics in color together with its interiors, this book shows in rich detail how Islam articulated itself architecturally, touching on historical and legendary memories and on themes of both religious harmony and Islamic triumph.","publicationYear":1996,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Buildings, structures","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Masjid al-Aqṣá (Jerusalem)","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Qubbat al-Ṣakhrah (Mosque : Jerusalem)","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Antiquities","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Islamic Architecture","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Jerusalem","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Jerusalem, history","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Jerusalem, antiquities","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar"}],"displaySummary":"From the time of Herod through the Crusades, Jerusalem had officially \"changed its religion\" several times, with Jews, Christians, and Muslims inscribing the story of their faiths on the urban landscape. In this handsomely illustrated book, noted Islamist Oleg Grabar offers a rare account of the great role played by early Islam in defining the \"look\" of Jerusalem that remained largely intact until the twentieth century. From about 640 to 1100, Muslims transformed Christian Jerusalem, mainly the area now known as the Haram al-Sharif, both physically and ideologically to embody their new faith. Grabar examines this process, showing how it led to great architectural achievements, including The Dome of the Rock, still perhaps the most vivid image to impress any visitor to Jerusalem. Offering a major photographic record of The Dome's mosaics in color together with its interiors, this book shows in rich detail how Islam articulated itself architecturally, touching on historical and legendary memories and on themes of both religious harmony and Islamic triumph.","primaryTopic":"Housing, Cities & Urban Life","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"History","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubject":"History","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores the historical transformation of Jerusalem during the early Islamic period, with a strong focus on the architectural developments and religious significance of the era.","id":"subject-evidence-book-the-shape-of-the-holy-early-islamic-jerusalem-oleg-grabar-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Islamic Architecture","mappedSubject":"Religion","score":6,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-libr